=========================================================
FACADE DESIGN PATTERN (STRUCTURAL DESIGN PATTERN)
=========================================================


1. DEFINITION
=========================================================

Facade Pattern is a Structural Design Pattern that provides a
simple unified interface to a complex subsystem.

It hides internal complexity and provides a single entry point
for the client.

One-Line Definition:

Facade provides a simplified interface to a set of subsystems.


---------------------------------------------------------


2. WHY DO WE NEED FACADE?
=========================================================

WITHOUT FACADE:

Client directly communicates with multiple subsystem classes.

Problems:

❌ Client knows subsystem details
❌ Client manages execution flow
❌ High coupling
❌ Difficult maintenance


Example:

        Client
          |
  ------------------
  |       |        |
Payment Inventory Email


---------------------------------------------------------

WITH FACADE:

Client communicates with only one object.

Benefits:

✔ Simple interface
✔ Facade manages workflow
✔ Reduced client complexity


Example:

             Client
                |
                |
             Facade
          /     |     \
         /      |      \
 Payment  Inventory  Email


---------------------------------------------------------


3. INTENT
=========================================================

Facade is used to:

✔ Simplify subsystem usage

✔ Hide implementation complexity

✔ Reduce client-subsystem dependency

✔ Provide a single entry point


---------------------------------------------------------


4. STRUCTURE
=========================================================


             Client

                |

                v

             Facade

        -----------------
        |       |       |

        v       v       v

    Payment Inventory Email



---------------------------------------------------------


5. COMPONENTS
=========================================================


1. Client

- Uses only Facade
- Does not directly interact with subsystems


2. Facade

- Provides simplified APIs
- Coordinates subsystem operations
- Hides complexity


3. Subsystems

- Perform actual business logic
- Independent classes


---------------------------------------------------------


6. RELATIONSHIP
=========================================================

Facade uses:

COMPOSITION (HAS-A)


Example:

OrderFacade HAS-A PaymentGateway

OrderFacade HAS-A Inventory

OrderFacade HAS-A InvoiceSystem

OrderFacade HAS-A EmailService


Facade does NOT modify subsystem behavior.

It only coordinates calls.


---------------------------------------------------------


7. BENEFITS
=========================================================


✔ Simplifies client code

✔ Reduces client coupling

✔ Hides subsystem complexity

✔ Centralizes workflow

✔ Easier maintenance


---------------------------------------------------------


8. DRAWBACKS
=========================================================


❌ Can become a God Class

❌ Additional abstraction layer

❌ May hide advanced subsystem functionality


---------------------------------------------------------


9. REAL WORLD EXAMPLES
=========================================================


1. Hotel Reception

Client:
Book Room()

Internally:

- Check availability
- Process payment
- Generate confirmation


2. Order Processing System

Client:

placeOrder()


Internally:

- Inventory check
- Payment
- Invoice
- Notification


3. Computer Startup

Client:

